WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:09.140
Now we're going to move on to just a couple of talks about various tools that you will be utilizing for gameplay code and systems code

2
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:14.140
that are kind of good to be aware of that don't really fit tidily into any of those previous brackets.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.140
So first off we have delegates and events.

4
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.579
First thing we should talk about is what actually is a delegate.

5
00:00:18.579 --> 00:00:24.579
This might be a kind of term and concept that you're already familiar with, especially if you've worked in C sharp because they've got them there.

6
00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:32.579
But if you're not aware of them, delegates are essentially a type that can reference and execute member functions on objects.

7
00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:41.579
So if you imagine a type that maintains an array of function pointers and it can maintain a reference to either a single function pointer or a set of them

8
00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:46.579
and then you can execute those functions from a single location, that's essentially what it is.

9
00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.579
It's an implementation of what would be called in formal terms the observer pattern.

10
00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:56.579
It's important to highlight one of the great powers and uses for these things is these delegates can be bound dynamically at runtime

11
00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:02.579
so you can change the functions that will get called back from a delegate.

12
00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:05.579
A classic example would be a switch in a video game.

13
00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:12.579
You as a designer might want a switch to open a door and then maybe one time you want to reuse that switch and have it turn on a light.

14
00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:17.579
And so if you do that by a delegate you're not having to really tightly couple these systems together.

15
00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:23.579
The light doesn't have to know or the switch doesn't have to know that it's going to be used on a light and a door.

16
00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:32.579
The switch just knows I'm telling people that I've been switched on and it's the people who are registering to that event that need to know about the light.

17
00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:35.319
Delegates are also safe to copy around.

18
00:01:35.319 --> 00:01:41.319
You can pass them around by value but it will allocate memory on the heap when you do this.

19
00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:48.319
So when possible, when it's not too difficult, passing by reference is probably a little bit more memory efficient.

20
00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:51.700
So there's a whole set of delegates available to us in Unreal.

21
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.700
The key ones to highlight is we have the concept of single versus multicast.

22
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.700
So single is a one-to-one binding.

23
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.700
You can only bind one function into this delegate at a time.

24
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.700
If you want to have a new function you need to remove the old one.

25
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.770
Multicast is kind of one-to-many bindings.

26
00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:15.770
So three, four, twenty functions could all link themselves, bind themselves to this one delegate.

27
00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:20.770
And when that delegate fires, all of those functions will get executed one after another.

28
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.150
And then we have the concept of dynamic.

29
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.150
There are both dynamic single and dynamic multicast delegates.

30
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:34.150
And dynamic essentially represents that they are available to the reflection system for a start.

31
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.150
So dynamic delegates can only be used on UObject and downwards types.

32
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.150
So ones that are kind of part of the Unreal ecosystem.

33
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:47.150
And they, particularly multicast dynamic delegates, can be exposed to Blueprints.

34
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.150
So that's one of the great uses for dynamic delegates.

35
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:54.150
Dynamic delegates are also serializable, meaning we can save them more easily.

36
00:02:54.150 --> 00:03:01.699
The kind of one that is something to bear in mind about dynamic versus non-dynamic is that dynamic do tend to be slower.

37
00:03:02.699 --> 00:03:04.699
So they're less kind of performant.

38
00:03:04.699 --> 00:03:08.699
And we have more capabilities available to us if we choose non-dynamic.

39
00:03:08.699 --> 00:03:16.699
So we can bind lambdas, we can bind function pointers to non-UObject types.

40
00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:22.699
And we can also use payloads, which we can't use for dynamic delegates.

41
00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:24.699
Which we'll get into a little bit more in a bit.

42
00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:26.699
There is also the concept of events.

43
00:03:26.699 --> 00:03:32.699
This is a kind of specialized type of delegate where the execution of that delegate is restricted to a particular class.

44
00:03:32.699 --> 00:03:38.699
So that delegate is owned by a particular class, its execution can only be fired by the class that owns that delegate.

45
00:03:38.699 --> 00:03:44.699
This still exists in code, but it's just worth mentioning that whilst it still exists and you can use it,

46
00:03:44.699 --> 00:03:51.699
actually that rule about only being owned by this particular type isn't actually strictly enforced.

47
00:03:51.699 --> 00:03:55.699
So from 4.2.6 onwards, it's deemed as essentially deprecated.

48
00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:58.699
So you might still find them referenced occasionally and used,

49
00:03:58.699 --> 00:04:03.699
but actually for the express purpose for which they're intended, it's not actually forced.

50
00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:06.659
You could kind of break that.

51
00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:10.659
So just some demonstrations for a single non-dynamic delegate.

52
00:04:10.659 --> 00:04:11.659
This is how it would look.

53
00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:15.659
Every time we declare a delegate, it would be in a format similar to this.

54
00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:17.660
So we'll get onto this in the following page with the other examples.

55
00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.660
You'll see this is kind of in common.

56
00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:24.660
These declarations always come with a declare underscore, it's a macro.

57
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:25.660
So declare delegate.

58
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.660
Retval here means this is a delegate that can return a value,

59
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.660
which is only applicable for single delegates because obviously if you had a multicast,

60
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.660
if five items bind to this delegate,

61
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.660
how would you know which of those five return values you're going to use?

62
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.660
So retval is only relevant when you're doing a non-multicast delegate.

63
00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.689
And one param means that this delegate takes one parameter.

64
00:04:48.689 --> 00:04:53.689
All forms of delegates can take up to, I think it's nine parameters they can have.

65
00:04:54.689 --> 00:05:02.779
There's a header you can actually look into, which is delegate combinations, I think it is,

66
00:05:02.779 --> 00:05:06.779
where you can just see all the different versions, iterations of this macro.

67
00:05:06.779 --> 00:05:09.779
But yeah, ultimately they can name conventions the same.

68
00:05:09.779 --> 00:05:12.779
So once you've written one, you'll kind of know how the rest are written.

69
00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:17.360
So what this does is this defines a signature for that delegate.

70
00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.360
And then we define an instance of that delegate to actually bind to and to broadcast from.

71
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.360
So this is defining the signature, and then this is the actual delegate that we're talking about.

72
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.360
And then in terms of actually using that delegate, this is an example of the code we might have.

73
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.360
So the kind of key things to highlight here is that because this is a non-dynamic delegate,

74
00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.360
again, we can bind to non-U object types.

75
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.360
So I've just created a temporary instance of this F some non-U object class

76
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.360
using shared pointers here, which we talked about in a previous session.

77
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.360
And this is just a little example of what that class actually looks like.

78
00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.360
So you can see we've just got this one function. It's really not very interesting class.

79
00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.360
It's just a kind of very minimal implementation.

80
00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.490
This function does need to be public so that we can bind to it.

81
00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:03.490
But if we look, the delegate says it's a one parameter delegate.

82
00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:08.490
And it's got a return value of int32 here we specified.

83
00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:12.490
So this function does return an int32, but it takes two parameters.

84
00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:15.490
So the reason that this is okay, the reason that you combine this function

85
00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:17.490
is because we're using something called payloads.

86
00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:23.490
So payload is essentially an ability to bind additional data to a delegate at bind time.

87
00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.490
So our delegate signature says it takes in one parameter.

88
00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.490
But if we want to bind to a function that takes additional parameters,

89
00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.490
we just need to say, and for those additional parameters, here's the data to feed into those.

90
00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.490
So at the point that we bind that function to that delegate,

91
00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.490
we're specifying what that additional data would be.

92
00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:45.490
So in this case, when we actually finally execute that delegate,

93
00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:48.490
which is what we're doing here, you can see because it has a return value,

94
00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.490
we can feed that value back into a variable and do whatever we want with that.

95
00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:57.519
We only execute and pass in the one variable that we specify here.

96
00:06:57.519 --> 00:06:59.519
This is our one parameter.

97
00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:02.519
But the other value that's going to be fed into this some other float

98
00:07:02.519 --> 00:07:05.519
has already been specified at the bind, and that's going to be this number seven.

99
00:07:05.519 --> 00:07:10.519
So when this function gets executed, it's going to receive 0.2 as infloat,

100
00:07:10.519 --> 00:07:13.519
and it's going to receive 7.0 as some other float.

101
00:07:13.519 --> 00:07:15.519
And then we can do whatever we want with that.

102
00:07:15.519 --> 00:07:21.870
The kind of things to mention here is that particularly when you're doing return values,

103
00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.870
we need to be sure that this delegate actually has something bound to it,

104
00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:28.870
so that's what this is bound is for.

105
00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:32.870
And when you're binding raw, when you're doing things that aren't UObjects,

106
00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:37.870
so we can't count on the fact that they're going to be properly garbage collected and cleaned up,

107
00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:39.870
when you're binding raw functions,

108
00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:43.870
it's possible that objects get deleted before you execute the delegate,

109
00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.870
which would cause a crash, and there's nothing protecting us from that.

110
00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:51.870
So you need to be quite diligent with making sure that when this object is getting destroyed,

111
00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.870
you make sure it unbinds from any delegates that it might be bound to.

112
00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:58.279
So then we move on to multicast.

113
00:07:58.279 --> 00:08:00.279
This is also the example of non-dynamic.

114
00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:04.279
So multicast, again, is our kind of one-to-many binding.

115
00:08:04.279 --> 00:08:06.279
Naming convention looks pretty similar.

116
00:08:06.279 --> 00:08:11.379
As you can see, declare multicast delegate and then our number of params.

117
00:08:11.379 --> 00:08:16.759
We don't have retval because multicast delegates can't return a return value.

118
00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:18.759
But the kind of thing I wanted to highlight here was,

119
00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:21.759
as you can see, we've got a delegate signature name,

120
00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:25.759
and then we have our two parameters, so this takes a bool and a UObject pointer.

121
00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:27.759
Now, this is defining a signature,

122
00:08:27.759 --> 00:08:31.759
but multiple instances could reuse that same signature,

123
00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:33.759
and you will see this in Unreal code sometimes,

124
00:08:33.759 --> 00:08:35.759
that we declare a signature once,

125
00:08:35.759 --> 00:08:38.759
and then you reuse that signature for different occasions.

126
00:08:38.759 --> 00:08:40.759
So you could imagine some situation where,

127
00:08:40.759 --> 00:08:45.759
if really all that we care about is what input parameters it takes,

128
00:08:45.759 --> 00:08:50.759
we don't need to do two, three, four different delegate declarations

129
00:08:50.759 --> 00:08:54.759
if we're going to have several types that essentially take the same input parameters.

130
00:08:54.759 --> 00:08:59.759
So we have an onHit object and an onDamagedBy object that both have the same signature,

131
00:08:59.759 --> 00:09:01.759
so we can reuse that delegate signature,

132
00:09:01.759 --> 00:09:03.759
even though these are different delegate instances

133
00:09:03.759 --> 00:09:05.759
and can be bound to independently.

134
00:09:05.759 --> 00:09:09.690
So when it actually comes to using this in code,

135
00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.690
the key things I wanted to highlight here is that

136
00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.690
when we are binding to a multicast delegate,

137
00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.690
rather than using the term bind, we use add,

138
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.690
because that's the concept, right?

139
00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.690
It's almost like an array of functions that it would call back to,

140
00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.690
so we use the term add.

141
00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:27.690
And because, again, this is non-dynamic,

142
00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.690
we have the ability to use lambdas.

143
00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.690
So for anyone who's not familiar with lambdas,

144
00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:33.690
that's also referred to as anonymous functions.

145
00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:36.690
It's just kind of like writing a function in line.

146
00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.690
So this is, I'm not going to go into detail about these things,

147
00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:43.690
but essentially this is, the body of this function would be,

148
00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.690
this isn't a function that's defined somewhere else in the class,

149
00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:49.139
it's defined here in line inside our body.

150
00:09:49.139 --> 00:09:52.139
And again, because it's multicast, we can add an additional function,

151
00:09:52.139 --> 00:09:56.139
and addUObject is the kind of simplest way

152
00:09:56.139 --> 00:10:00.139
to add a reference to a function that is part of a UObject,

153
00:10:00.139 --> 00:10:02.139
so a non-raw object,

154
00:10:02.139 --> 00:10:05.139
and then that means it's going to be automatically garbage collected

155
00:10:05.139 --> 00:10:06.139
and it's a little bit safer.

156
00:10:06.139 --> 00:10:09.139
If you try and bind a raw function to an object

157
00:10:09.139 --> 00:10:11.139
that is actually a UObject or inherits from that chain,

158
00:10:11.139 --> 00:10:15.139
so in this case, this is our actor which inherits from object,

159
00:10:15.139 --> 00:10:17.139
you'll actually get told off by the compiler,

160
00:10:17.139 --> 00:10:18.139
so it won't actually let you do that.

161
00:10:18.139 --> 00:10:23.139
So you have to use addUObject if it's a UObject type or downwards.

162
00:10:23.139 --> 00:10:25.970
And then you can just see here,

163
00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:29.970
we just declared a null value just to kind of have some parameter

164
00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:31.970
to feed in to this delegate.

165
00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.970
And we can perform that same check here for isBound,

166
00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.970
it's not necessary, but it's tidy.

167
00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.970
And then instead of execute,

168
00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.970
we use the term broadcast for multicast delegates

169
00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:45.970
because that's kind of more indicative of what it's doing,

170
00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:47.970
it's letting possibly several things know,

171
00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:49.970
it's broadcasting this information.

172
00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:54.480
And then last of all, we actually have the dynamic delegates.

173
00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.480
Like I said, you can have non-multicast dynamic delegates,

174
00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.480
but if you want this to be exposed to Blueprint,

175
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.480
it does need to be multicast.

176
00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.480
So whenever you make a delegate,

177
00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:08.480
or I think they're called event dispatches in Blueprint,

178
00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.480
that is always a multicast dynamic delegate.

179
00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:14.480
So this is what that syntax would look like,

180
00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.480
very, very similar to the previous ones, as you can see,

181
00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.480
but it's declared dynamic, then multicast,

182
00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.480
or we wouldn't have multicast there if it was a single one,

183
00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.480
declared dynamic multicast delegate,

184
00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:25.480
and then our number of prams.

185
00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.480
Again, because this is multicast,

186
00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.610
it can't have a return value.

187
00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.610
If you do want to expose it to Blueprint,

188
00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:33.610
it does have to be dynamic multicast,

189
00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.610
but also you need this additional metadata

190
00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.610
above your delegate instance.

191
00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.610
So in this case, you property,

192
00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.610
because even though delegates kind of call two functions,

193
00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:47.610
they are actually properties, they are data,

194
00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.610
and you use this blueprint assignable.

195
00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:53.610
If we didn't have this above our delegate instance,

196
00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:55.610
then it wouldn't be visible to Blueprint.

197
00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.700
Then in terms of actually binding and using it,

198
00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:59.700
we have very similar functions

199
00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.700
to our previous non-dynamic multicasts,

200
00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.700
where it's using the term add,

201
00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:06.700
and then it's add dynamic.

202
00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:09.700
So we can use add dynamic or add unique dynamic

203
00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:10.700
if you want to make sure

204
00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:12.700
that you haven't already bound this function.

205
00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:13.700
When you're typing this in,

206
00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.700
it's just worth highlighting that actually sometimes

207
00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:18.700
your IDE will kind of do some weird things

208
00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.700
where it says it can't find the function,

209
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.700
but if you just type add unique dynamic,

210
00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:25.700
it will actually realize that that's okay.

211
00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:27.700
It might try and prompt you to preface it

212
00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.700
with some underscores, but that's not necessary.

213
00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.700
And then otherwise, it looked pretty much the same

214
00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.700
as our U object bindings,

215
00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.700
where we just specified the object

216
00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:37.700
that the function is getting called on,

217
00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:40.700
and then which function we're actually binding to.

218
00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:42.700
Obviously, this function signature

219
00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:44.700
needs to match the signature of our delegate

220
00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.700
as the case with all the others.

221
00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:47.700
As I mentioned previously,

222
00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.700
we can't use payloads here,

223
00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:52.700
so we can't use bind time data

224
00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:53.700
to make functions

225
00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.700
that don't actually have the same signature

226
00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:56.700
match our delegate signature,

227
00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:58.700
so just worth bearing that in mind.

228
00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:00.700
And then we have these same checks available to us

229
00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:01.700
where we can check whether or not

230
00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.700
that delegate has anything bound to it

231
00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:07.700
and then that same terminology broadcast for our value.

232
00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:11.700
Now, we don't have to bind Blueprint functions

233
00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:12.700
to this delegate.

234
00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.700
We can also bind C++ functions,

235
00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.700
but the thing that's worth highlighting about that

236
00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:18.700
is that if it's a dynamic delegate,

237
00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:22.700
that function has to have a U function metadata on it.

238
00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.700
If you tried to bind a function

239
00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:27.700
to a dynamic delegate without U function,

240
00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.700
it would probably not cause a compile error,

241
00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.700
but when you go to broadcast that,

242
00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:32.700
it would tell you off,

243
00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:34.700
so that's just something to bear in mind.

244
00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:35.700
And then last of all,

245
00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:39.740
this is just what it would look like in Blueprint.

246
00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:41.740
The one thing I haven't actually touched on here

247
00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:45.740
is that with our delegate signature declaration,

248
00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:48.740
with dynamic delegates, you specify for each parameter,

249
00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:49.740
you specify the parameter type,

250
00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.740
and then also a default name for that parameter.

251
00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:54.740
And that's not relevant here.

252
00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:56.740
You can see these names don't match up.

253
00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.740
In C++, you can call it whatever you like,

254
00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:01.740
but in Blueprint, if you go to bind to that delegate,

255
00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.740
automatically it will use this default name

256
00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:04.740
as your parameter name.

257
00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:06.740
So I have not typed that in.

258
00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:09.740
I just dragged off this event and typed custom event

259
00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:10.740
and it auto-generated this

260
00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.740
and it used float parameter name

261
00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:15.740
as the default name for that parameter.

262
00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:17.740
So that's the kind of difference

263
00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:21.740
that you'll notice between delegate signature declarations

264
00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:22.740
is with non-dynamics,

265
00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:25.740
you don't specify a name per parameter,

266
00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:26.740
but for dynamic delegates,

267
00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:28.740
you do have to do an additional argument

268
00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.740
that is a name for each parameter.

269
00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:32.740
So if this were two parameters,

270
00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:34.740
it would be float, float param name,

271
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.860
bool, bool param name or whatever.

272
00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.860
So that about wraps it up for delegates.
